    {
      "question_id": 11,
      "question": "Which factors should be considered when choosing a chunk size?",
      "type": "multi_hop",
      "tags": [
        "list",
        "spans_pages",
        "project_relevant"
      ],
      "expected": "answerable",
      "trace_id": "936bbc0a2770",
      "action": "answered",
      "refused": false,
      "answer": "When deciding on a chunk size, you should consider:\n\n1. **Task’s preference** – Different tasks need different granularity.  \n   * Question‑answering usually works with short chunks (≈128 tokens).  \n   * Summarization often requires longer chunks (≈512 tokens).\n\n2. **Encoder’s preference** – The model’s maximum input length and its performance on short vs. long texts.  \n   * Sentence‑transformer models perform best on single sentences.  \n   * Models like text‑embedding‑ada‑002 handle longer texts better.  \n   * The chunk size is usually limited by the encoder’s max sequence length (e.g., 512 tokens for BERT‑based models).\n\n3. **Query’s preference** – The length of user queries should match the chunk size so that the amount of context in chunks aligns with that in queries; otherwise retrieval relevance can suffer.\n\nThese three factors—task, encoder, and query—guide the choice of chunk size, and there is no single “golden rule” that applies to all RAG scenarios.",
